<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-mathematical-writing-problem-solving">
<title>Mathematical Writing and Problem Solving</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Familiarity with mathematical notation, sets, functions, logic, and proof
methods from the preceding sections of Chapter 0.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>read mathematical writing actively rather than mechanically;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use definitions as working mathematical tools;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>write mathematical expressions as parts of complete sentences;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>introduce variables and notation clearly;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>organize displayed equations and derivations effectively;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>translate between mathematical notation and spoken language;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify the givens, unknowns, conditions, and goals of a problem;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply a systematic problem-solving process;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use diagrams, tables, patterns, and special cases strategically;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>work both forward from known information and backward from a goal;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use estimation, units, domains, and special cases to check results;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish exploratory work from a polished mathematical solution;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>write clear proofs and worked solutions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize common weaknesses in mathematical communication.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-mathematics-as-communication">
<title>Mathematics as Communication</title>
<p>Mathematics is not merely a collection of calculations.</p>

<p>It is a language for expressing definitions, relationships, patterns,
arguments, structures, and conclusions precisely.</p>

<p>A correct calculation can still be difficult to understand if its notation is
unclear or its reasoning is poorly organized.</p>

<p>Likewise, a well-written mathematical argument allows another reader to
reconstruct not only what was done, but why each step is valid.</p>

<p>\begin{conceptbox}
Good mathematical writing aims for three qualities:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><em>correctness</em> --- every statement is mathematically valid;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><em>clarity</em> --- the reader can understand what each symbol and step means;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><em>precision</em> --- assumptions, domains, definitions, and conclusions are stated accurately.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>\end{conceptbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-reading-mathematics-actively">
<title>Reading Mathematics Actively</title>
<p>Mathematical reading is usually slower than ordinary reading.</p>

<p>A single displayed equation may contain several relationships that must be
interpreted before continuing.</p>

<p>When reading mathematics, ask:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>What objects are being discussed?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>What do the symbols represent?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>What is assumed?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>What is being claimed?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Which definitions are being used?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Why does one step follow from the previous step?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Is the statement an example, definition, theorem, or conclusion?</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Reading actively often requires stopping and reconstructing omitted
intermediate reasoning.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-reading-symbols-as-meaning">
<title>Reading Symbols as Meaning</title>
<p>Symbols should not be read merely as shapes.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>x\in A</md>
</p>

<p>should be understood as</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ is an element of @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>A\subseteq B</md>
</p>

<p>means</p>

<p><q>every element of @@PROTECTED0@@ is also an element of @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>The symbolic form is compact, but the underlying meaning should remain
accessible.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-reading-mathematical-structure">
<title>Reading Mathematical Structure</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}.</md>
</p>

<p>A reader should notice more than the numerator and denominator.</p>

<p>The expression has a restriction:</p>

<p>
<md>x\neq1.</md>
</p>

<p>The numerator factors:</p>

<p>
<md>x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus, for <m>x\neq1</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x+1.</md>
</p>

<p>The restriction remains important even after simplification.</p>

<p>Active reading identifies both algebraic structure and domain information.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-definitions-working-tools">
<title>Definitions Are Working Tools</title>
<p>Definitions should not be treated as vocabulary to memorize and then ignore.</p>

<p>They tell us exactly what must be shown.</p>

<p>Suppose one wants to prove that a function is injective.</p>

<p>The definition says that one must establish</p>

<p>
<md>f(x_1)=f(x_2)\Longrightarrow x_1=x_2.</md>
</p>

<p>That definition immediately suggests the structure of the proof.</p>

<p>Similarly, to prove</p>

<p>
<md>A\subseteq B,</md>
</p>

<p>the definition tells us to begin with an arbitrary</p>

<p>
<md>x\in A</md>
</p>

<p>and establish</p>

<p>
<md>x\in B.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-unpacking-definitions">
<title>Unpacking Definitions</title>
<p>A useful mathematical habit is to replace terminology with its precise
definition.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|l}
\text{Term} &amp; \text{Useful Mathematical Form}\\ \hline
n\text{ is even} &amp; n=2k\text{ for some }k\in\Z\\
n\text{ is odd} &amp; n=2k+1\text{ for some }k\in\Z\\
A\subseteq B &amp; x\in A\Rightarrow x\in B\\
f\text{ injective} &amp; f(x_1)=f(x_2)\Rightarrow x_1=x_2\\
f\text{ surjective} &amp; \forall y\in B,\;\exists x\in A,\;f(x)=y
\end{array}</md>
</p>

<p>Definitions convert abstract words into usable mathematical conditions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-mathematical-sentences">
<title>Mathematical Sentences</title>
<p>Mathematical expressions should usually function as parts of grammatical
sentences.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>Since <m>x&gt;0</m>, we have <m>x^2&gt;0</m>.</p>

<p>This is preferable to an unexplained sequence such as</p>

<p>
<md>x&gt;0,\qquad x^2&gt;0.</md>
</p>

<p>The second version contains mathematical information but does not communicate
the logical relationship between the statements.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-symbols-not-explanation">
<title>Symbols Do Not Replace Explanation</title>
<p>Symbolic notation is powerful because it compresses information.</p>

<p>However, excessive symbolic compression can obscure reasoning.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>x\in A\Rightarrow x\in B\Rightarrow A\subseteq B</md>
</p>

<p>may be understandable in context, but a proof is clearer when the role of the
arbitrary element is stated:</p>

<p>Let <m>x\in A</m> be arbitrary. If <m>x\in A</m>, then <m>x\in B</m>. Therefore every
element of <m>A</m> belongs to <m>B</m>, so <m>A\subseteq B</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-introducing-variables">
<title>Introducing Variables</title>
<p>A variable should be introduced before it is used.</p>

<p>Instead of writing</p>

<p>
<md>n=2k</md>
</p>

<p>without context, write:</p>

<p>Let <m>n</m> be an even integer. Then <m>n=2k</m> for some <m>k\in\Z</m>.</p>

<p>The reader now knows:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>what <m>n</m> represents;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>why <m>k</m> exists;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>what set <m>k</m> belongs to.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-declare-domain">
<title>Declare the Domain</title>
<p>A variable's domain can fundamentally change the meaning of a statement.</p>

<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>x^2=2.</md>
</p>

<p>Over <m>\R</m>, this equation has solutions</p>

<p>
<md>x=\pm\sqrt2.</md>
</p>

<p>Over <m>\Q</m>, it has no solutions.</p>

<p>Therefore statements such as</p>

<p><q>Let @@PROTECTED0@@ be a real number</q></p>

<p>or</p>

<p><q>Let @@PROTECTED0@@</q></p>

<p>often carry essential mathematical information.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-introduce-specialized-notation">
<title>Introduce Specialized Notation</title>
<p>When notation is created for a local argument, explain it.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>Let <m>E</m> denote the set of even integers.</p>

<p>Then one may write</p>

<p>
<md>E=\{2k:k\in\Z\}.</md>
</p>

<p>The notation should help the reader rather than force the reader to guess its
meaning.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-notation-consistency">
<title>Use Notation Consistently</title>
<p>Once a symbol has been assigned a meaning, that meaning should remain stable
throughout the argument unless explicitly redefined.</p>

<p>If <m>n</m> denotes an integer at the beginning of a proof, it should not later
become a real-valued function without explanation.</p>

<p>Consistency reduces unnecessary cognitive effort.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-displayed-mathematics">
<title>Displayed Mathematics</title>
<p>Important equations are often placed on their own line:</p>

<p>
<md>ax+b=0.</md>
</p>

<p>Displayed mathematics is useful when:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>an equation is central to the discussion;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>several transformations must be shown;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the expression is too long for comfortable inline reading;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the reader needs to refer visually to the equation.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Short expressions can remain inline when displaying them would interrupt the
flow unnecessarily.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-aligned-derivations">
<title>Aligned Derivations</title>
<p>When several equations form one calculation, alignment can reveal the
structure.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\begin{aligned}
(x+3)^2
&amp;=(x+3)(x+3)\\
&amp;=x^2+3x+3x+9\\
&amp;=x^2+6x+9.
\end{aligned}</md>
</p>

<p>The equality signs align vertically, making the chain of equivalent
expressions easy to follow.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-justifying-steps">
<title>Justifying Important Steps</title>
<p>Not every elementary algebraic step needs a paragraph of explanation.</p>

<p>However, important transitions should be justified.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>a^2=b^2</md>
</p>

<p>does not imply only</p>

<p>
<md>a=b.</md>
</p>

<p>The correct conclusion over the real numbers is</p>

<p>
<md>a=b\qquad\text{or}\qquad a=-b.</md>
</p>

<p>A written solution should make such logical branches visible.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-equality-sign-writing">
<title>Equality Signs Must Mean Equality</title>
<p>The equality symbol should connect expressions that are actually equal.</p>

<p>A common poor habit is to write</p>

<p>
<md>x^2-4=0=x^2=4=x=2.</md>
</p>

<p>This is not a valid chain of equalities.</p>

<p>A clearer derivation is</p>

<p>
<md>\begin{aligned}
x^2-4&amp;=0\\
x^2&amp;=4\\
x&amp;=\pm2.
\end{aligned}</md>
</p>

<p>Each line now follows meaningfully from the previous one.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-problem-solving-process">
<title>The Problem-Solving Process</title>
<p>Problem solving is rarely a perfectly linear process.</p>

<p>Nevertheless, a useful general framework is:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>understand the problem;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>organize the information;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>choose a strategy;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>carry out the strategy;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>check the result;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>communicate the solution.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>These stages may repeat.</p>

<p>A failed strategy often provides information that suggests a better one.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-understand-problem">
<title>Step 1: Understand the Problem</title>
<p>Before calculating, determine what the problem actually asks.</p>

<p>Identify:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>the known quantities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the unknown quantities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the assumptions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the domain;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>any restrictions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the desired conclusion.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>A surprising number of errors begin with solving a different problem from the
one that was asked.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-identify-goal">
<title>Identify the Goal</title>
<p>Different goals require different forms of answers.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><q>Solve</q> asks for values satisfying a condition.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>Prove</q> asks for a logically complete argument.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>Show that</q> generally asks for proof or derivation.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>Find</q> asks for a specific mathematical object or value.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>Determine whether</q> requires a decision together with justification.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>Give a counterexample</q> asks for one example disproving a universal claim.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Understanding the verb in the problem is part of understanding the problem.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-organize-information">
<title>Step 2: Organize the Information</title>
<p>Rewrite the given information in a useful form.</p>

<p>A verbal statement may become an equation.</p>

<p>A geometric problem may become a diagram.</p>

<p>A collection of numerical observations may become a table.</p>

<p>A logical condition may become an implication.</p>

<p>A complicated expression may be factored or rewritten.</p>

<p>Organization often reveals structure that was hidden in the original wording.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-choose-strategy">
<title>Step 3: Choose a Strategy</title>
<p>Possible strategies include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>applying a definition;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>drawing a diagram;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>making a table;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>introducing variables;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>writing an equation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>simplifying an expression;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>factoring;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>working backward;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>considering special cases;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>searching for a pattern;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>breaking the problem into smaller parts;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>using a known theorem;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>using contradiction or contraposition;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>constructing an example;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>looking for a counterexample.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Choosing a strategy is often the most creative part of mathematics.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-carry-out-strategy">
<title>Step 4: Carry Out the Strategy</title>
<p>Once a strategy is selected, execute it carefully.</p>

<p>Keep track of:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>algebraic restrictions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>domains;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>signs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>units;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>logical assumptions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>whether transformations are reversible.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>A correct strategy can still fail through careless execution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-check-result">
<title>Step 5: Check the Result</title>
<p>A solution should be checked whenever possible.</p>

<p>Useful checks include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>substitution into the original equation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>checking units;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>estimating magnitude;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>checking signs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>checking the domain;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>considering special cases;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>testing boundary values;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>comparing with known behavior.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Checking does not replace proof, but it is an important tool for detecting
errors.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-communicate-solution">
<title>Step 6: Communicate the Solution</title>
<p>A finished solution should usually be more organized than the scratch work
used to discover it.</p>

<p>The final presentation should contain:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>the essential reasoning;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>enough intermediate work to verify the result;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>definitions or theorems when needed;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>a clearly identified conclusion.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Exploration may be messy.</p>

<p>Presentation should not be.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-complete-problem-solving">
<title>Worked Example: A Complete Problem-Solving Process</title>
<example>
<title>Solve and Check a Rational Equation</title>
<p>Solve</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{2}{x-1}=3.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>x=\frac53</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-work-forward">
<title>Work Forward</title>
<p>Working forward means beginning with known information and determining what
follows.</p>

<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>x&gt;3.</md>
</p>

<p>Then one may deduce</p>

<p>
<md>x+2&gt;5.</md>
</p>

<p>If <m>x</m> is known to be positive, one may also deduce</p>

<p>
<md>x^2&gt;0.</md>
</p>

<p>Forward reasoning asks:</p>

<p><q>What can I conclude from what I already know?</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-work-backward">
<title>Work Backward</title>
<p>Working backward begins with the desired conclusion and asks what would be
sufficient to establish it.</p>

<p>Suppose the goal is</p>

<p>
<md>(x-2)^2=0.</md>
</p>

<p>One may ask:</p>

<p>What condition would make this true?</p>

<p>Clearly,</p>

<p>
<md>x-2=0</md>
</p>

<p>would be sufficient, which suggests</p>

<p>
<md>x=2.</md>
</p>

<p>Backward reasoning is especially useful during problem discovery.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-meet-in-middle">
<title>Meet in the Middle</title>
<p>Many difficult problems are solved by combining forward and backward
reasoning.</p>

<p>From the hypotheses, derive useful consequences.</p>

<p>From the goal, determine what intermediate result would be sufficient.</p>

<p>Then search for a connection between the two.</p>

<p>This strategy is common in proofs, geometry, algebra, and analysis.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-break-problem-smaller">
<title>Break a Problem into Smaller Parts</title>
<p>A complicated problem often becomes manageable when decomposed.</p>

<p>For example, to analyze</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\frac{x^2-4}{x^2-x-6},</md>
</p>

<p>one might separately:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>factor the numerator;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>factor the denominator;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>determine domain restrictions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>simplify common factors;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret the remaining expression.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>Each smaller task is easier than handling the entire expression at once.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-draw-diagram">
<title>Draw a Diagram</title>
<p>A diagram can reveal relationships that are difficult to see symbolically.</p>

<p>Diagrams are particularly useful in:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>geometry;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>trigonometry;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>vector analysis;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>graph theory;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>probability;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>optimization;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>multivariable calculus.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>A diagram should support reasoning rather than replace it.</p>

<p>A picture may suggest that something is true without proving it.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-make-table">
<title>Make a Table</title>
<p>Tables organize repeated calculations and can reveal patterns.</p>

<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>a_n=n^2-n.</md>
</p>

<p>Evaluating several values gives</p>

<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
n&amp;1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;4&amp;5\\ \hline
a_n&amp;0&amp;2&amp;6&amp;12&amp;20
\end{array}</md>
</p>

<p>The values suggest</p>

<p>
<md>a_n=n(n-1),</md>
</p>

<p>which is always even because one of two consecutive integers is even.</p>

<p>The table helps discover the pattern; a proof establishes it.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-search-patterns">
<title>Search for Patterns</title>
<p>Patterns often suggest conjectures.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>1=1^2,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>1+3=4=2^2,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>1+3+5=9=3^2,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>1+3+5+7=16=4^2.</md>
</p>

<p>This suggests</p>

<p>
<md>1+3+\cdots+(2n-1)=n^2.</md>
</p>

<p>The observed pattern motivates the statement.</p>

<p>Mathematical induction can then prove it.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-problem-solving-conjectures">
<title>Conjectures</title>
<p>A <em>conjecture</em> is a mathematical statement believed to be true based on
evidence or reasoning but not yet established by proof.</p>

<p>Experimentation is valuable for producing conjectures.</p>

<p>However,</p>

<p>\begin{importantbox}
A pattern suggests a theorem; it does not prove a theorem.
\end{importantbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-test-special-cases">
<title>Test Special Cases</title>
<p>Special cases can clarify a general problem.</p>

<p>If a statement involves a parameter <m>n</m>, test values such as</p>

<p>
<md>n=1,\qquad n=2,\qquad n=3.</md>
</p>

<p>If it involves a real variable, useful cases may include</p>

<p>
<md>x=0,\qquad x=1,\qquad x=-1.</md>
</p>

<p>Special cases can:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>reveal errors;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>suggest patterns;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify missing assumptions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>produce counterexamples.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-look-counterexamples">
<title>Look for Counterexamples</title>
<p>Before attempting to prove a statement, ask whether it is actually true.</p>

<p>Consider the claim</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{x^2}=x.</md>
</p>

<p>Testing</p>

<p>
<md>x=-3</md>
</p>

<p>gives</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{(-3)^2}=3\neq-3.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the claim is false.</p>

<p>The correct identity is</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{x^2}=|x|.</md>
</p>

<p>A small test can prevent a long attempt to prove a false statement.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-estimate-before-calculating">
<title>Estimate Before Calculating</title>
<p>Estimation gives a rough expectation for the answer.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>19.8(5.1)</md>
</p>

<p>should be close to</p>

<p>
<md>20(5)=100.</md>
</p>

<p>If a calculator returns</p>

<p>
<md>10.098,</md>
</p>

<p>the result should immediately appear suspicious.</p>

<p>Estimation provides a simple error-detection mechanism.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-exact-approximate-writing">
<title>Exact and Approximate Answers</title>
<p>Whenever practical, preserve exact values until approximation is needed.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt2</md>
</p>

<p>is exact, whereas</p>

<p>
<md>1.414</md>
</p>

<p>is approximate.</p>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\approx</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>is approximately equal to.</q></p>

<p>Thus one may write</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt2\approx1.414.</md>
</p>

<p>Using <m>=</m> instead of <m>\approx</m> would incorrectly claim exact equality.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-check-units">
<title>Check Units</title>
<p>Units provide mathematical information.</p>

<p>Suppose distance is measured in meters and time in seconds.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{\text{distance}}{\text{time}}</md>
</p>

<p>has units</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{\mathrm m}{\mathrm s}.</md>
</p>

<p>A proposed speed with units</p>

<p>
<md>\mathrm m^2</md>
</p>

<p>would therefore signal an error.</p>

<p>This type of reasoning is called <em>dimensional analysis</em> in many applied
contexts.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-unit-consistency">
<title>Unit Consistency</title>
<p>Quantities being added or subtracted should have compatible units.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>3\text{ meters}+5\text{ meters}=8\text{ meters}</md>
</p>

<p>is meaningful.</p>

<p>But</p>

<p>
<md>3\text{ meters}+5\text{ seconds}</md>
</p>

<p>does not represent an ordinary physical sum.</p>

<p>Unit consistency becomes especially important in applied mathematics,
physics, engineering, and mathematical modeling.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-check-domain">
<title>Check the Domain</title>
<p>Algebraic manipulation may produce values that are not allowed in the
original problem.</p>

<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{x+1}{x-2}=0.</md>
</p>

<p>The denominator requires</p>

<p>
<md>x\neq2.</md>
</p>

<p>The equation is zero when the numerator is zero:</p>

<p>
<md>x=-1.</md>
</p>

<p>The domain restriction must remain part of the reasoning.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-extraneous-solutions">
<title>Extraneous Solutions</title>
<p>Some operations can introduce candidate solutions that do not satisfy the
original equation.</p>

<p>For example, squaring both sides is not always reversible.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>x=-2,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>x^2=4.</md>
</p>

<p>But starting from</p>

<p>
<md>x^2=4</md>
</p>

<p>does not allow one to conclude only <m>x=-2</m>; both signs are possible.</p>

<p>In more complicated equations, squaring can produce extraneous solutions.</p>

<p>Therefore candidate solutions should often be checked in the original
equation.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-reversible-steps">
<title>Reversible and Irreversible Steps</title>
<p>An equivalence transformation preserves exactly the same solution set.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>x+3=7\Longleftrightarrow x=4.</md>
</p>

<p>However, an implication may preserve only one direction.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>x=2\Longrightarrow x^2=4,</md>
</p>

<p>but</p>

<p>
<md>x^2=4\not\Longrightarrow x=2.</md>
</p>

<p>The distinction between implication and equivalence is important when solving
equations and writing proofs.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-reading-advanced-notation">
<title>Reading Advanced Notation</title>
<p>As mathematics becomes more advanced, notation becomes increasingly compact.</p>

<p>The goal should remain to understand the mathematical sentence represented by
the symbols.</p>

<p>For example, later in calculus one encounters</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L.</md>
</p>

<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\lim</md>
</p>

<p>is read <q>limit.</q></p>

<p>In this context, the arrow</p>

<p>
<md>x\to a</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ approaches @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>the limit of @@PROTECTED0@@ as @@PROTECTED1@@ approaches @@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>

<p>The complete statement</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>the limit of @@PROTECTED0@@ as @@PROTECTED1@@ approaches @@PROTECTED2@@ equals @@PROTECTED3@@.</q></p>

<p>The rigorous meaning of limits is developed later in Analysis.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-context-symbol-reading">
<title>Context Determines How Symbols Are Read</title>
<p>The same symbol can have different readings in different mathematical
contexts.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ implies @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>For a function,</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ maps @@PROTECTED1@@ to @@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>

<p>In calculus,</p>

<p>
<md>x\to a</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ approaches @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>The shape of a symbol alone does not determine its interpretation.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-writing-proofs-clearly">
<title>Writing Proofs Clearly</title>
<p>A proof should communicate its logical structure.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p>Let <m>n</m> be an even integer. By definition, <m>n=2k</m> for some <m>k\in\Z</m>.
Then</p>

<p>
<md>n^2=(2k)^2=4k^2=2(2k^2).</md>
</p>

<p>Since <m>2k^2\in\Z</m>, <m>n^2</m> is even.</p>

<p>The proof contains:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>an arbitrary object;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the relevant definition;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the calculation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the final connection back to the definition.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-do-not-hide-conclusion">
<title>Do Not Hide the Conclusion</title>
<p>A proof should clearly indicate when the desired result has been established.</p>

<p>Phrases such as</p>

<p><q>Therefore @@PROTECTED0@@ is even</q></p>

<p>or</p>

<p><q>Hence @@PROTECTED0@@</q></p>

<p>help the reader connect the final step with the original claim.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-writing-worked-solutions">
<title>Writing Worked Solutions</title>
<p>A good worked solution should answer three questions:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>What are we trying to find?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Why is each important step valid?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>What is the final answer?</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>The amount of explanation depends on the problem and intended audience.</p>

<p>Elementary algebra may require more intermediate steps than an advanced
analysis calculation.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-improving-solution">
<title>Worked Example: Improving a Solution</title>
<example>
<title>Write a Clear Solution</title>
<p>Solve</p>

<p>
<md>4(x-3)+5=17.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>x=6</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-exploration-vs-presentation">
<title>Exploration versus Presentation</title>
<p>The process of discovering mathematics is often messy.</p>

<p>Scratch work may include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>failed approaches;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>numerical experiments;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>diagrams;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>guesses;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>incomplete calculations;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>rewritten expressions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>These are valuable parts of problem solving.</p>

<p>However, the final solution should usually present the successful argument in
a logical order.</p>

<p>\begin{conceptbox}
Discovery and presentation serve different purposes.</p>

<p>Exploration helps the mathematician find the solution.</p>

<p>Presentation helps the reader understand the solution.
\end{conceptbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-when-strategy-fails">
<title>When a Strategy Fails</title>
<p>A failed strategy is not necessarily wasted effort.</p>

<p>It may reveal:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>which information is insufficient;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>which assumptions matter;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>which definitions should be used;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>which cases behave differently;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>whether the statement may be false.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Problem solving is often iterative rather than immediate.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-technology-problem-solving">
<title>Use Technology as a Tool</title>
<p>Calculators, computer algebra systems, numerical software, graphing programs,
and programming languages can assist mathematical exploration.</p>

<p>They can help:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>calculate examples;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>visualize functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>test conjectures;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>approximate solutions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>simulate mathematical models;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>manipulate large datasets.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>However, computational output should still be interpreted mathematically.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-computation-not-proof">
<title>Computation Is Not Automatically Proof</title>
<p>Suppose a computer checks a statement for the first million positive integers.</p>

<p>This is strong evidence.</p>

<p>It does not generally prove the statement for every positive integer.</p>

<p>Similarly, a graph may suggest that two functions intersect once, but the
display alone may not establish uniqueness.</p>

<p>Technology supports reasoning; it does not automatically replace it.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-common-writing-problems">
<title>Common Mathematical Writing Problems</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-undefined-variables">
<title>Undefined Variables</title>
<p>Poor:</p>

<p>
<md>x^2+y^2=r^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Better:</p>

<p>Let <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> denote the legs of a right triangle and let <m>r</m> denote
the length of its hypotenuse. Then</p>

<p>
<md>x^2+y^2=r^2.</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-ambiguous-pronouns">
<title>Ambiguous Pronouns</title>
<p>Statements such as</p>

<p><q>This shows it is true</q></p>

<p>may be unclear when several objects have just been discussed.</p>

<p>Name the object or conclusion explicitly.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-unexplained-equations">
<title>Unexplained Equations</title>
<p>A long chain of equations without explanation may leave the reader unable to
identify the purpose of the calculation.</p>

<p>Use brief prose to identify important steps.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-too-much-explanation">
<title>Too Much Explanation</title>
<p>Clarity does not require explaining every trivial arithmetic operation.</p>

<p>Writing</p>

<p><q>Now add @@PROTECTED0@@ to @@PROTECTED1@@, which produces @@PROTECTED2@@</q></p>

<p>may be unnecessary in an advanced text.</p>

<p>The appropriate level of explanation depends on the mathematical difficulty
and intended audience.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-incorrect-equality-writing">
<title>Incorrect Use of Equality</title>
<p>Do not use <m>=</m> to mean:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><q>therefore</q>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>approximately</q>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>the next step is</q>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>implies.</q></p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Each of these ideas has its own mathematical or linguistic expression.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-ignoring-restrictions-writing">
<title>Ignoring Restrictions</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\frac1{x-3},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>x\neq3</md>
</p>

<p>is part of the mathematical information.</p>

<p>A solution that ignores the restriction is incomplete.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-premature-rounding">
<title>Premature Decimal Approximation</title>
<p>Replacing exact values by rounded decimals too early can introduce accumulated
error.</p>

<p>For example, it is usually preferable to retain</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{\sqrt2}{3}</md>
</p>

<p>during symbolic work and approximate only when a decimal answer is required.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-problem-solving-checklist">
<title>A General Problem-Solving Checklist</title>
<p>Before considering a solution complete, ask:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>What exactly was asked?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Did I identify all assumptions and restrictions?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Are my variables clearly defined?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Did I use the correct domain?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Is each important step justified?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Did I accidentally divide by zero or apply an invalid operation?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Are the units consistent?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Is the magnitude reasonable?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Did I check possible extraneous solutions?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Does the final answer actually answer the original question?</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-diagnose-solution">
<title>Worked Example: Diagnose an Incorrect Solution</title>
<example>
<title>Find the Error</title>
<p>A student solves</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{x+6}=x</md>
</p>

<p>by squaring:</p>

<p>
<md>x+6=x^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Rearranging gives</p>

<p>
<md>x^2-x-6=0,</md>
</p>

<p>so</p>

<p>
<md>(x-3)(x+2)=0.</md>
</p>

<p>The candidate solutions are</p>

<p>
<md>x=3\qquad\text{and}\qquad x=-2.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>x=3</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-translate-solving">
<title>Worked Example: Translate Before Solving</title>
<example>
<title>Translate a Verbal Problem</title>
<p>A rectangle has length <m>3</m> meters greater than its width and perimeter
<m>30</m> meters. Find its dimensions.</p>

<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>w=\text{width}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\ell=w+3,</md>
</p>

<p>where the symbol <m>\ell</m> is the lowercase Greek letter <em>ell</em> used here
to represent length and is read <q>ell.</q></p>

<p>The perimeter equation is</p>

<p>
<md>2\ell+2w=30.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>The rectangle is <m>6</m> meters wide and <m>9</m> meters long.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-mathematical-writing-exercises">
<title>Exercises</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Rewrite the following as a complete mathematical sentence:</p>

<p>
<md>x&gt;4,\qquad x^2&gt;16.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain in words what</p>

<p>
<md>x\in A\cap B</md>
</p>

<p>means.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Read aloud:</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Read aloud:</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in\R,\;x^2\geq0.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Read aloud:</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt2=1.414</md>
</p>

<p>is not mathematically exact. Rewrite it correctly.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Identify the domain restriction in</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\frac{x+2}{x-7}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain what is wrong with the chain</p>

<p>
<md>x^2=9=x=3.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Solve and check:</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{3}{x+2}=6.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Solve and check:</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{x+5}=x-1.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A rectangle has length <m>5</m> units greater than its width and perimeter
<m>42</m> units. Define variables, write an equation, and determine the
dimensions.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A car travels <m>180</m> miles in <m>3</m> hours. Determine its average speed and
include the correct units.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Before calculating exactly, estimate</p>

<p>
<md>49.7(2.03).</md>
</p>

<p>Then calculate and compare.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Consider the claim</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{a+b}=\sqrt a+\sqrt b.</md>
</p>

<p>Test special cases and determine whether the statement is always true.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Consider the sequence</p>

<p>
<md>2,6,12,20,30,\ldots.</md>
</p>

<p>Compute several related expressions and propose a formula for the <m>n</m>-th
term.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why checking the first <m>1000</m> cases of a universal statement does
not generally constitute a proof.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Write a clear proof that the sum of two odd integers is even. Introduce every
variable and state the relevant definitions.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Rewrite the following argument more clearly:</p>

<p>
<md>n=2k+1,\quad m=2j+1,\quad n+m=2k+2j+2=2(k+j+1),\quad\text{even}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the transformation</p>

<p>
<md>x=3\Longrightarrow x^2=9</md>
</p>

<p>is valid but not reversible without additional possibilities.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A calculation produces a length of <m>-4</m> meters. Explain why the mathematical
context should cause the result to be reconsidered.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A speed calculation produces units of</p>

<p>
<md>\mathrm{m}\cdot\mathrm{s}.</md>
</p>

<p>Explain why dimensional analysis suggests an error.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Develop a conjecture from the pattern</p>

<p>
<md>1=1,\qquad 1+2=3,\qquad 1+2+3=6,\qquad 1+2+3+4=10.</md>
</p>

<p>Then state what additional work would be needed to turn the conjecture into a
theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose a computer verifies a number-theoretic claim for every integer from
<m>1</m> through <m>10^9</m>. Explain what this establishes and what it does not
establish.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Choose a theorem from <xref ref="sec-methods-of-proof" />. Identify its hypotheses,
conclusion, definitions used, and overall proof strategy.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-foundations-final-connections">
<title>Chapter 0 Connections</title>
<remark>
<title>Chapter Connection</title>
<p>This section brings together the major ideas of Chapter 0. Mathematical
notation provides the language; arithmetic and number systems provide basic
objects and operations; set theory organizes collections; relations and
functions describe mathematical structure; logic formalizes statements;
proof methods establish truth; and cardinality extends the idea of size to
infinite sets. These tools form the common foundation for the branches of
mathematics developed throughout the remainder of this text.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-foundations-looking-ahead">
<title>Looking Ahead</title>
<p>The remainder of this text develops increasingly specialized mathematical
structures, but the habits established in Chapter 0 remain essential.</p>

<p>When encountering a new concept:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>identify the objects being studied;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>learn the definitions precisely;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand the notation and how it is read;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>study examples and counterexamples;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify the central theorems;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand why those theorems are true;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>practice applying them to new problems;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>connect the new ideas to previously established mathematics.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>These habits turn mathematical study from memorizing isolated formulas into
understanding connected structures.</p>
</subsection>

</section>
